ORGO.reg({"types":{"chair-read":{"id":"chair-read","title":"A chair is drawn: cis or trans, is it the most stable, and if not draw the one that is","exam":"Q4A","points":50,"asks":"A disubstituted cyclohexane is drawn as a chair. Say cis or trans, say whether it is the most stable conformation, and if not draw the most stable one.","know":[{"h":"A chair has two kinds of positions. The six <b>axial</b> bonds are vertical. The six <b>equatorial</b> bonds point out around the middle of the ring, each one parallel to the ring bonds one bond away.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1; Lecture 10/20, p. 3"},{"h":"Axial bonds <b>alternate</b> up, down, up, down around the ring. On every carbon the equatorial bond points to the opposite face from the axial bond: where the axial bond is up, the equatorial bond is the down one.","from":"standard; Exam key Q4A"},{"h":"<b>cis</b> means both groups on the same face of the ring (both up or both down). <b>trans</b> means opposite faces. Up and down are about faces, not about axial and equatorial.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 4; standard"},{"h":"<b>1,2 pattern</b>: one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. Two equatorial neighbours look as if they point the same way, but they are trans.","from":"Exam key Q4A"},{"h":"<b>1,3 pattern</b> (the reverse of 1,2): two equatorial, or two axial, is cis. One axial and one equatorial is trans.","from":"standard"},{"h":"<b>1,4 pattern</b> (the same as 1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans.","from":"Exam key Q4A"},{"h":"The <b>ring flip</b> turns every axial group equatorial and every equatorial group axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so cis stays cis and trans stays trans.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1"},{"h":"<b>Axial groups cost energy</b>: an axial group has two gauche interactions with the ring. An equatorial group has none.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 2"},{"h":"The numbers to know (cost of an axial group, kcal/mol, and K): methyl 1.74 (19.5); ethyl 1.79 (21.2); propyl 2.21 (43.4); isopropyl 2.61 (86.0); tert-butyl 5.5 (11,916). The larger the group, the more it costs.","from":"Table 5.3"},{"h":"The most stable chair has the most groups equatorial. If one group must be axial, the <b>larger group goes equatorial</b>.","from":"Exam key Q4A"},{"h":"Size here means crowding near the ring, not the size of the atom. Halogens, including iodine, and OH cost less when axial than any alkyl group, because a single atom on a long bond stays clear of the ring. COOH and {{NH2}} cost a little less than methyl.","from":"A-value table (literature values); Exam key Q4A"},{"h":"Skill: be able to redraw a chair after the ring flip, with each group on the same carbon and the same face, and with no wedges or dashes.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1"}],"method":["For each group, decide whether its bond is vertical (axial) or points outward (equatorial).","For each group, decide whether it is up or down: an axial group points straight up or down, and an equatorial group points to the face opposite the axial bond on its carbon.","Same face is cis, opposite faces is trans. Check against the pattern for 1,2, 1,3 or 1,4.","Picture the ring flip: every axial group becomes equatorial and the reverse.","Compare the two chairs: both equatorial beats everything, and when one group must be axial the larger group should be equatorial.","If the drawn chair is not the better one, draw the flipped chair with each group on the same carbon and the same face."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Calling two equatorial neighbours cis because both seem to point sideways. They are trans."},{"h":"Using the 1,2 pattern for a 1,3 pair. At 1,3 two equatorial groups are cis."},{"h":"Changing cis to trans when drawing the ring flip. The flip never changes up and down."},{"h":"Putting the group with the biggest atom equatorial. Iodine or bromine costs less when axial than methyl does."},{"h":"Saying a chair with one axial group cannot be the most stable. For a cis-1,2, trans-1,3 or cis-1,4 pair one group is always axial."}],"links":{"read":["rings-2"]}}},"items":{"chair-read-001":{"id":"chair-read-001","type":"chair-read","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH(CH3)2,2u:Cl","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH(CH3)2,2u:Cl|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Isopropyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Cl on C2 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial isopropyl points straight up. At C2 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: Cl is <b>up</b>.","ask":{"q":"At C2 the axial bond points down. Is the equatorial bond there the up one or the down one?","a":"The up one. The two bonds on a ring carbon always point to opposite faces."}},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face or on opposite faces?","a":"The same face: cis."}},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial Cl would cost about 0.53 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, isopropyl, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one.","ask":{"q":"Which of the two groups costs more when it is axial?","a":"Isopropyl. It is the one that should be equatorial."}},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1u:CH(CH3)2,2u:Cl|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: isopropyl equatorial, Cl axial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-002":{"id":"chair-read-002","type":"chair-read","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(1)","subs":"1u:NH2,2d:OCH3","given":"chairB","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:NH2,2d:OCH3|B]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"{{NH2}} on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. {{OCH3}} on C2 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: {{NH2}} is <b>up</b>. At C2 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: {{OCH3}} is <b>down</b>.","ask":{"q":"At C1 the axial bond points down. Is the equatorial bond there the up one or the down one?","a":"The up one. The two bonds on a ring carbon always point to opposite faces."}},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. This is the classic trap: two equatorial neighbours both seem to point sideways, but one is up and one is down. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. One axial and one equatorial is cis.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face or on opposite faces?","a":"Opposite faces: trans."}},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one.","ask":{"q":"What would the ring flip do to these two groups?","a":"Make both axial, which costs energy. So the drawn chair is the better one."}}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,2: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans."}},"chair-read-003":{"id":"chair-read-003","type":"chair-read","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,3u:OH","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3u:OH|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Methyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. OH on C3 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial methyl points straight up. The axial OH points straight up.","ask":{"q":"If the axial bond at one carbon points up, which way does the axial bond at the next carbon point?","a":"Down. Axial bonds alternate around the ring."}},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. Two axial groups two carbons apart point the same way and sit close together on one face of the ring. For 1,3: two equatorial, or two axial, is cis. One axial and one equatorial is trans.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face or on opposite faces?","a":"The same face: cis."}},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are axial. Axial groups cost energy: 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for methyl and about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups) for OH, and at 1,3 the two axial groups also crowd each other. The ring flip puts both equatorial, where they cost nothing. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one.","ask":{"q":"What would the ring flip do to these two groups?","a":"Make both equatorial, which removes all the axial cost."}},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3u:OH|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip, with both groups equatorial.","rule":"1,3: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is cis; axial and equatorial is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-004":{"id":"chair-read-004","type":"chair-read","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(3)","subs":"1u:I,4u:CH2CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:I,4u:CH2CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"I on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Ethyl on C4 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial I points straight up. At C4 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: ethyl is <b>up</b>.","ask":{"q":"At C4 the axial bond points down. Is the equatorial bond there the up one or the down one?","a":"The up one. The two bonds on a ring carbon always point to opposite faces."}},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. For 1,4: one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face or on opposite faces?","a":"The same face: cis."}},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial I costs about 0.47 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). Axial ethyl would cost 1.79 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The larger group, ethyl, is already equatorial. This chair is the most stable one. Do not judge by the size of the atom alone: iodine is a big atom, but its long C-I bond holds it away from the ring, so it costs little when axial. Ethyl is the larger group here.","ask":{"q":"Which of the two groups costs more when it is axial?","a":"Ethyl. It is the one that should be equatorial."}}],"answer":"cis; yes, this is the most stable chair (the larger group, ethyl, is equatorial).","rule":"1,4: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-005":{"id":"chair-read-005","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(4)","subs":"1d:NH2,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:NH2,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"{{NH2}} on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. Br on C2 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: {{NH2}} is <b>down</b>. At C2 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: Br is <b>up</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. This is the classic trap: two equatorial neighbours both seem to point sideways, but one is up and one is down. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. One axial and one equatorial is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,2: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans."}},"chair-read-006":{"id":"chair-read-006","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(2)","subs":"1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"OH on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. Isopropyl on C2 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: OH is <b>down</b>. The axial isopropyl points straight down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are down: same face, so <b>cis</b>. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial OH would cost about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, isopropyl, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: isopropyl equatorial, OH axial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-007":{"id":"chair-read-007","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(5)","subs":"1u:COOH,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"COOH on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Br on C2 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial COOH points straight up. At C2 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: Br is <b>up</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial COOH costs about 1.4 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). Axial Br would cost about 0.5 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, COOH, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one. COOH is the larger group: a carbon with two oxygens on it, against a single atom on a long bond."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: COOH equatorial, Br axial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-008":{"id":"chair-read-008","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,4d:CH2CH3","given":"chairB","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,4d:CH2CH3|B]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Methyl on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. Ethyl on C4 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: methyl is <b>up</b>. At C4 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: ethyl is <b>down</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. For 1,4: two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. One axial and one equatorial is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,4: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-009":{"id":"chair-read-009","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1d:CH3,3d:CH(CH3)2","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:CH3,3d:CH(CH3)2|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Methyl on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. Isopropyl on C3 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: methyl is <b>down</b>. At C3 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: isopropyl is <b>down</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are down: same face, so <b>cis</b>. Do not carry the 1,2 habit over: two equatorial groups at 1,3 are on the same face. For 1,3: two equatorial, or two axial, is cis. One axial and one equatorial is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"cis; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,3: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is cis; axial and equatorial is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-010":{"id":"chair-read-010","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:C(CH3)3,2u:CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:C(CH3)3,2u:CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"tert-butyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Methyl on C2 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial tert-butyl points straight up. At C2 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: methyl is <b>up</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial tert-butyl costs 5.5 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial methyl would cost 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The larger group, tert-butyl, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one. An axial tert-butyl group is the most expensive one in the lecture table (K = 11,916 in favour of equatorial), so this chair is almost never seen."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1u:C(CH3)3,2u:CH3|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: tert-butyl equatorial, methyl axial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-011":{"id":"chair-read-011","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,2d:CH2CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:CH2CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Methyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Ethyl on C2 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial methyl points straight up. The axial ethyl points straight down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. Two axial neighbours point in opposite directions, one straight up and one straight down. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. One axial and one equatorial is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are axial. Axial groups cost energy: 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for methyl and 1.79 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for ethyl. The ring flip puts both equatorial, where they cost nothing. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still trans.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:CH2CH3|B]]"}],"answer":"trans; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip, with both groups equatorial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-012":{"id":"chair-read-012","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,3d:C(CH3)3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3d:C(CH3)3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Methyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. tert-butyl on C3 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial methyl points straight up. At C3 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: tert-butyl is <b>down</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. For 1,3: one axial and one equatorial is trans. Two equatorial, or two axial, is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial tert-butyl would cost 5.5 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The larger group, tert-butyl, is already equatorial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (the larger group, tert-butyl, is equatorial).","rule":"1,3: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is cis; axial and equatorial is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-013":{"id":"chair-read-013","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH(CH3)2,3d:F","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:CH(CH3)2,3d:F|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Isopropyl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. F on C3 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial isopropyl points straight up. At C3 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: F is <b>down</b>."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. For 1,3: one axial and one equatorial is trans. Two equatorial, or two axial, is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial F would cost about 0.3 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, isopropyl, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still trans.\n[[chair:1u:CH(CH3)2,3d:F|B]]"}],"answer":"trans; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: isopropyl equatorial, F axial.","rule":"1,3: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is cis; axial and equatorial is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-014":{"id":"chair-read-014","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1d:Ph,4d:OH","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:Ph,4d:OH|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Phenyl on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. OH on C4 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points up, so the equatorial bond is the down one: phenyl is <b>down</b>. The axial OH points straight down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are down: same face, so <b>cis</b>. For 1,4: one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial OH costs about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). Axial phenyl would cost about 2.8 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, phenyl, is already equatorial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"cis; yes, this is the most stable chair (the larger group, phenyl, is equatorial).","rule":"1,4: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-015":{"id":"chair-read-015","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:Cl,4d:CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:Cl,4d:CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Cl on C1 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>. Methyl on C4 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. The axial Cl points straight up. The axial methyl points straight down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group is up and one is down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. For 1,4: two equatorial, or two axial, is trans. One axial and one equatorial is cis."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"Both groups are axial. Axial groups cost energy: about 0.53 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups) for Cl and 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for methyl. The ring flip puts both equatorial, where they cost nothing. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still trans.\n[[chair:1u:Cl,4d:CH3|B]]"}],"answer":"trans; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip, with both groups equatorial.","rule":"1,4: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"chair-read-016":{"id":"chair-read-016","type":"chair-read","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:Br,2u:CH3","given":"chairB","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:Br,2u:CH3|B]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Br on C1 points outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away: <b>equatorial</b>. Methyl on C2 is on a vertical bond: <b>axial</b>."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Every ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring. At C1 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond is the up one: Br is <b>up</b>. The axial methyl points straight up."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Both groups are up: same face, so <b>cis</b>. On neighbouring carbons (1,2): one axial and one equatorial is cis. Two equatorial, or two axial, is trans."},{"t":"Most stable?","h":"One group must be axial in either chair, so the question is which. Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial Br would cost about 0.5 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups). The larger group, methyl, is axial here. This chair is <b>not</b> the most stable one. The trap is the big bromine atom: its long C-Br bond keeps it away from the ring, so methyl is the group that needs the equatorial position."},{"t":"Draw the ring flip","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group becomes axial. Up stays up and down stays down, so the molecule is still cis.\n[[chair:1u:Br,2u:CH3|A]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The most stable chair is the ring flip: methyl equatorial, Br axial.","rule":"1,2: axial and equatorial is cis; equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans. Larger group equatorial."}},"exam-q4a-1":{"id":"exam-q4a-1","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(1)","subs":"1u:NH2,2d:OCH3","given":"chairB","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:NH2,2d:OCH3|B]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Axial bonds are vertical. Equatorial bonds point outward, roughly parallel to the ring bonds one bond away. Here {{NH2}} and {{OCH3}} are both equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"Each ring carbon has one bond that points more up and one that points more down. At a carbon whose axial bond points up, the equatorial bond is the down one, and the reverse. Axial bonds alternate up, down, up around the ring, so on two neighbouring carbons the equatorial bonds are one up and one down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"One group up, one down: opposite faces, so <b>trans</b>. This is the classic trap: two equatorial groups on neighbouring carbons both seem to point sideways, but they are trans."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"Both groups are equatorial, so there is no axial group to pay for. The ring flip would make both axial. This chair is the most stable one."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"1,2: equatorial and equatorial (or axial and axial) is trans."}},"exam-q4a-2":{"id":"exam-q4a-2","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(2)","subs":"1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"OH is equatorial. The isopropyl group is on a vertical bond: axial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial isopropyl points down. At the neighbouring carbon the axial bond points up, so its equatorial bond, the one that holds OH, is the down one. Both groups are down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. A cis-1,2 pair always has one axial and one equatorial group."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"No. The larger group should be equatorial, and here the larger group, isopropyl, is axial. In the lecture's table an axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol, the largest value after tert-butyl. An OH is much smaller."},{"t":"Draw the better chair","h":"Flip the ring: every axial group becomes equatorial and every equatorial group axial. Up and down do not change, so it is still cis.\n[[chair:1d:OH,2d:CH(CH3)2|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The more stable chair is the flipped one, with isopropyl equatorial and OH axial.","rule":"cis-1,2: one axial, one equatorial. Put the larger group equatorial."}},"exam-q4a-3":{"id":"exam-q4a-3","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(3)","subs":"1u:I,4u:CH2CH3","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:I,4u:CH2CH3|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"Iodine is axial (vertical bond). The ethyl group is equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial iodine points up. Carbons 1 and 4 are across the ring from each other, and their axial bonds point in opposite directions: at C4 the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond that holds the ethyl group is the up one. Both groups are up."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. A cis-1,4 pair, like a cis-1,2 pair, always has one axial and one equatorial group."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"One group has to be axial, so the question is which. Ethyl costs 1.79 kcal/mol when axial (lecture table). Iodine is a single atom on a long C-I bond and costs much less (tables give about 0.5). The larger group, ethyl, is already equatorial: this is the most stable chair."}],"answer":"cis; yes, this is the most stable chair (the larger group, ethyl, is equatorial).","rule":"A halogen is a small axial group. A carbon group wants the equatorial position more."}},"exam-q4a-4":{"id":"exam-q4a-4","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(4)","subs":"1d:NH2,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1d:NH2,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"{{NH2}} and Br are both equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"On neighbouring carbons the axial bonds point in opposite directions, so the equatorial bonds do too. One equatorial group is up and the other is down."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"Both groups are equatorial. The flipped chair would have both axial. This is the most stable chair."}],"answer":"trans; yes, this is the most stable chair (both groups equatorial).","rule":"Two equatorial groups on neighbouring carbons are trans, and that chair is the stable one."}},"exam-q4a-5":{"id":"exam-q4a-5","type":"chair-read","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q4A(5)","subs":"1u:COOH,2u:Br","given":"chairA","ask":["cis-trans","stable"],"stem":"Are the two groups cis or trans? Is this the most stable conformation? If it is not, draw the one that is.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|A]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Axial or equatorial","h":"COOH is axial (vertical bond). Br is equatorial."},{"t":"Up or down","h":"The axial COOH points up. At the neighbouring carbon the axial bond points down, so the equatorial bond that holds Br is the up one. Both groups are up."},{"t":"cis or trans","h":"Same face: <b>cis</b>. One axial, one equatorial, as always for cis-1,2."},{"t":"Most stable","h":"No. COOH is the larger group: a carbon with two oxygens on it, against a single bromine atom on a long C-Br bond (tables give about 1.4 kcal/mol for an axial COOH and about 0.5 for an axial Br). The larger group should be equatorial."},{"t":"Draw the better chair","h":"Flip the ring: COOH becomes equatorial and Br axial. Both are still up, still cis.\n[[chair:1u:COOH,2u:Br|B]]"}],"answer":"cis; no. The more stable chair is the flipped one, with COOH equatorial and Br axial.","rule":"When one group must be axial, let it be the smaller one. Halogens are small."}}},"mols":{}});
